from deep2 import d, DEEP

d("xiii-1", "Cyril, Methodius and the Slavonic liturgy",
 "In 862 Prince Rastislav of Great Moravia asked Constantinople for teachers who could instruct his people in their own language, partly to reduce the influence of Frankish clergy. Emperor Michael III and Patriarch Photius sent two brothers from Thessalonica, Constantine (who took the monastic name Cyril shortly before his death) and Methodius, who knew the Slavonic dialect of the Thessalonica region.",
 "Constantine created the Glagolitic alphabet and translated the Gospels and liturgical texts. Frankish bishops objected that only Hebrew, Greek and Latin were fit for worship. The brothers went to Rome, where Pope Hadrian II approved the Slavonic liturgy (c. 868). Cyril died in Rome in 869; Methodius was consecrated archbishop of Sirmium, imprisoned for over two years by Bavarian bishops, and after his death in 885 his disciples were expelled from Moravia.",
 "The work survived in Bulgaria. Boris I had been baptized around 864–865 and, after flirting with Rome, chose Constantinople. Disciples such as Clement and Naum founded schools at Ohrid and Preslav, and the Cyrillic alphabet, based on Greek letters, was developed there in the late ninth or early tenth century. Old Church Slavonic became the liturgical and literary language of Bulgaria, Serbia and Rus'.",
 "Serbia's church became autocephalous under Saint Sava in 1219. The Slavic churches inherited Byzantine theology and liturgy but in their own language, which made Orthodoxy a central part of national identity in the Balkans and Eastern Europe. John Paul II named Cyril and Methodius co-patrons of Europe in 1980.")

d("xiii-2", "From Kiev to Moscow to the Synodal period",
 "The Rus' Primary Chronicle tells how Prince Vladimir of Kiev sent envoys to examine Islam, Judaism, Latin Christianity and Byzantine worship, and how the envoys reported of Hagia Sophia that 'we knew not whether we were in heaven or on earth'. The story is literary, but the baptism of Vladimir and of the people of Kiev around 988, connected with his marriage to the emperor's sister Anna, is historical. The Metropolitan of Kiev was appointed from Constantinople and was usually Greek until the fifteenth century.",
 "The Mongol conquest (1237–1240) devastated Kiev. The Mongols generally exempted the Church from taxes. The metropolitan moved to Vladimir and then, in 1325, to Moscow, whose princes rose to dominate the region. Monastic revival led by Sergius of Radonezh (d. 1392) spread monasteries into the northern forests.",
 "Moscow rejected the Union of Florence, deposed the metropolitan Isidore who had signed it, and in 1448 elected its own metropolitan without Constantinople's approval: in effect, autocephaly. After 1453 some Russian writers spoke of Moscow as the 'Third Rome'. Constantinople recognised a Patriarchate of Moscow in 1589.",
 "Patriarch Nikon's reforms in the 1650s brought Russian liturgical books and practices into line with contemporary Greek usage (for example, making the sign of the cross with three fingers instead of two). Councils in 1666–1667 condemned those who refused, the Old Believers, who were persecuted and divided into many groups. Peter the Great abolished the patriarchate and in 1721 placed the Church under a Holy Synod supervised by a lay official, the Ober-Procurator. The patriarchate was restored only in 1917, weeks before the Bolshevik seizure of power.")

d("xiii-3", "The Church under the Ottoman sultans",
 "After taking Constantinople in 1453, Mehmed II installed the anti-unionist monk Gennadios Scholarios as patriarch in 1454. Over time the patriarch became head of the Orthodox community (Rum millet) in civil as well as religious matters: he answered to the sultan for his flock, collected taxes and judged family law. The Armenian patriarch of Constantinople had a similar role for non-Chalcedonian Christians.",
 "Christians were dhimmis: protected but subordinate, paying the poll-tax (jizya), forbidden to build new churches without permission, and barred from carrying arms. Through the devshirme, Christian boys from the Balkans were taken for the Janissary corps and administration, mainly between the fifteenth and seventeenth centuries. Conversion to Islam was common in some regions, such as Bosnia and Albania. Christians who converted to Islam and returned to Christianity could be executed; the Church honours many of them as 'neomartyrs'.",
 "The patriarchate became dependent on the Ottoman court and on wealthy Greek families of the Phanar district, and patriarchs were frequently deposed. The eighteenth century saw the Kollyvades movement and the publication of the Philokalia (Venice, 1782), an anthology of hesychast texts that later shaped Russian and modern Orthodox spirituality.",
 "Nineteenth-century nationalism broke the millet into national churches as states won independence: Greece declared autocephaly in 1833 (recognised 1850), Serbia in 1879, Romania in 1885. The Bulgarian Exarchate, created by an Ottoman decree in 1870, was declared schismatic by Constantinople in 1872 on the ground of 'ethnophyletism', the organisation of the Church by nationality. The schism was healed only in 1945.")

d("xiii-4", "Four ancient churches on the edges of empires",
 "Georgia. Tradition credits Saint Nino, a woman from Cappadocia, with converting the royal court of Kartli (Iberia) in the 330s. The Georgian Church followed the Armenians in rejecting Chalcedon for a time but reaccepted it around 607. Its catholicos gained autocephaly in the early medieval period; it was absorbed into the Russian Church in 1811 and restored its autocephaly in 1917.",
 "Armenia. The monk Mesrop Mashtots created the Armenian alphabet around 405 to translate the Bible. At the battle of Avarayr (451) Armenians fought the Persians to defend their faith. Armenia did not attend Chalcedon and later rejected it, partly in reaction to Byzantine pressure. The catholicos at Etchmiadzin heads the Armenian Apostolic Church, with a second catholicosate at Antelias (Cilicia) in Lebanon.",
 "Egypt. After 451 the majority of Egyptian Christians rejected Chalcedon and followed their own patriarchs. The Arab conquest (639–642) ended Byzantine interference. Over centuries Arabic replaced Coptic as the spoken language, and Christians became a minority, probably by the tenth to fourteenth centuries. The Coptic Orthodox Church today is the largest Christian community in the Middle East.",
 "Ethiopia. Frumentius, a Syrian captive at the court of Aksum, converted King Ezana in the mid-fourth century and was consecrated bishop by Athanasius. Ezana's coins replaced pagan symbols with the cross. Monks known as the Nine Saints spread monasticism around 500. The rock-hewn churches of Lalibela date from around 1200. For centuries the Ethiopian Church's metropolitan (abuna) was an Egyptian monk sent from Alexandria; the church became autocephalous with its own patriarch in 1959. Its canon of Scripture is the largest of any church and includes 1 Enoch and Jubilees.")

d("xiii-6", "How the Eastern Catholic Churches came about",
 "Eastern Catholic Churches are communities that keep an Eastern liturgy, canon law and spiritual tradition while in full communion with Rome. There are now 23 of them (sui iuris). Most arose from unions with groups of Orthodox or Oriental Christians from the sixteenth century onward; the Maronites (XIII.5) and the Italo-Albanians claim never to have broken communion.",
 "Key unions include: the Chaldean Catholic Church, from a group of the Church of the East that elected a patriarch in union with Rome in 1552 (the line became stable in 1830); the Union of Brest (1595–1596), in which most Orthodox bishops of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth accepted Rome, creating the Ruthenian (now Ukrainian Greek Catholic) Church; the Union of Uzhhorod (1646); the Romanian Greek Catholics (1698–1700); the Melkite Greek Catholics after a disputed patriarchal election in Antioch in 1724; the Armenian Catholics (1742); the Syriac Catholics (1783); and the Syro-Malankara Catholics in India (1930).",
 "For the Orthodox these unions, called 'uniatism', were experienced as division of their churches and often as coercion, especially where state power was involved. In the other direction, Eastern Catholics in the Soviet Union and Romania were forcibly merged into Orthodox churches after 1945, the Ukrainian Greek Catholic Church in 1946, and were legalised again only in 1989–1990, which led to bitter disputes over church buildings.",
 "Vatican II's decree Orientalium Ecclesiarum (1964) affirmed that Eastern Catholics should preserve their own traditions and called latinisation a distortion. The Code of Canons of the Eastern Churches (1990) gave them their own law. The Balamand statement (1993) accepted their existence but rejected uniatism as a model for future reunion.")

d("xiii-7", "How the Thomas Christians divided",
 "The Christians of Kerala trace their origin to the apostle Thomas, said to have arrived in 52. The tradition cannot be verified, but Christian communities on the Malabar coast are attested by the sixth century, when the traveller Cosmas Indicopleustes mentions them. A tradition of migration under Thomas of Cana (variously dated to the fourth or eighth century) explains the origin of the Knanaya community. Copper plates granted by local rulers in the ninth century show the Christians as a privileged merchant group. For centuries they received bishops from the Church of the East and used East Syriac liturgy.",
 "The Portuguese arrived in 1498. Archbishop Aleixo de Menezes of Goa held the Synod of Diamper (Udayamperur) in 1599, which brought the community under Latin jurisdiction, condemned 'Nestorian' books and latinised its practices. Resentment led to the Coonan Cross Oath of 1653, in which many swore never to submit to the Jesuits.",
 "Those who returned to Rome became the Syro-Malabar Church, now one of the largest Eastern Catholic Churches, with its own major archbishop. The rest received a bishop from the Syriac Orthodox patriarch of Antioch in 1665 and adopted the West Syriac liturgy. They later divided into the Malankara Orthodox Syrian Church (autocephalous catholicos) and the Jacobite Syrian Christian Church (under the patriarch of Antioch), whose property dispute has been in Indian courts for a century.",
 "Further groups include the Mar Thoma Church, reformed under Anglican influence in the nineteenth century; the Syro-Malankara Catholic Church (1930); the Chaldean Syrian Church of the East in Thrissur; and the Malabar Independent Syrian Church. In all, the Thomas tradition is now shared by at least seven churches.")

d("xiv-1", "Galileo, and the 'conflict' story",
 "Copernicus published his heliocentric system in 1543 with a dedication to Paul III, and for decades it raised little official concern. In 1616 the Congregation of the Index suspended his book 'until corrected' and declared heliocentrism contrary to Scripture, and Cardinal Bellarmine instructed Galileo not to hold or defend it. Bellarmine had written that if a true demonstration were found, Scripture would have to be reinterpreted, but that none had been given.",
 "In 1632 Galileo published the Dialogue Concerning the Two Chief World Systems, with permission from the censors. It was read as a defence of Copernicanism and as mocking Urban VIII, formerly his friend, through the character Simplicio. In 1633 the Roman Inquisition found him 'vehemently suspect of heresy', required him to abjure and sentenced him to house arrest, where he wrote his Two New Sciences. The famous words 'and yet it moves' are first reported more than a century later.",
 "The ban on heliocentric books was eased in 1758 and dropped in 1822–1835. In 1992 John Paul II accepted the conclusions of a commission that the theologians who condemned Galileo had erred.",
 "Historians of science now reject the 'conflict thesis' popularised in the nineteenth century by John Draper and Andrew Dickson White, which presented the history of science and religion as a constant war. Many early modern scientists, such as Kepler, Boyle and Newton, were devout, and the Galileo case involved scientific uncertainty, Counter-Reformation anxieties about private interpretation of Scripture, and personal and political factors.")

d("xiv-2", "Toleration and the revolutions",
 "Arguments for toleration grew from the experience of religious war. Sebastian Castellio protested against Servetus' execution; the Dutch Republic tolerated dissenters in practice; Roger Williams founded Rhode Island (1636) on liberty of conscience. John Locke's Letter Concerning Toleration (1689) argued that the state's role is limited to civil interests and that belief cannot be compelled, though he excluded atheists and Catholics. England's Toleration Act (1689) allowed Protestant dissenters to worship.",
 "Enlightenment thinkers criticised revealed religion in different degrees. Deists accepted a creator and natural morality but rejected miracles and special revelation. Voltaire campaigned against clerical intolerance ('écrasez l'infâme'); Hume questioned miracles and natural theology; Kant redefined religion within the limits of reason. Many Enlightenment figures, especially in Britain, Germany and America, remained Christians.",
 "The United States separated church and state at the federal level in the First Amendment (1791), though several states kept established churches into the early nineteenth century; Massachusetts was the last, in 1833.",
 "The French Revolution nationalised church property (1789), imposed the Civil Constitution of the Clergy (1790), which split the clergy between jurors and non-jurors, and in 1793–1794 attempted 'dechristianisation', closing churches and promoting the Cult of Reason and the Cult of the Supreme Being. Thousands of priests were killed or deported. Napoleon's Concordat with Pius VII (1801) restored Catholic worship under state control. The experience shaped the nineteenth-century Catholic Church's deep suspicion of liberalism.")

d("xiv-3", "Heart religion: Pietism, Methodism and revival",
 "Lutheran Pietism began with Philipp Jakob Spener's Pia Desideria (1675), which called for small groups for Bible study (collegia pietatis), a more active laity and a practical, heartfelt faith. August Hermann Francke turned Halle into a centre of education, an orphanage and missions. Count Nikolaus von Zinzendorf received Moravian refugees on his estate, which became Herrnhut (1722); a renewal there in 1727 launched one of the first Protestant missionary movements.",
 "In England, John Wesley, an Anglican priest, was influenced by Moravians, and at Aldersgate in May 1738 'felt his heart strangely warmed'. With his brother Charles, whose hymns spread the movement's theology, and George Whitefield, he preached in the open air to working people. Wesley organised converts into societies, classes and bands, and used lay preachers. He taught an Arminian theology of universal grace and Christian perfection. Methodism separated from the Church of England after his death; in America it became a separate church in 1784.",
 "In the American colonies the Great Awakening of the 1730s–1740s, associated with Jonathan Edwards and Whitefield, split Congregational and Presbyterian churches into supporters and critics of revival. A Second Great Awakening (c. 1790–1840) included camp meetings such as Cane Ridge, Kentucky (1801), and Charles Finney's 'new measures'. It made Methodists and Baptists the largest American denominations and fed reform movements including abolition and temperance.",
 "Revivalism defined evangelical Protestantism: the emphasis on personal conversion, the Bible, the cross and active mission (David Bebbington's four marks of evangelicalism).")

d("xiv-5", "Nineteenth-century new movements",
 "The nineteenth century, especially in the United States, produced several movements that drew on Christianity but redefined it. Joseph Smith published the Book of Mormon in 1830 and founded the Church of Christ, later the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints; after Smith's murder in 1844 most members followed Brigham Young to Utah. The Millerites expected Christ's return in 1844; out of the 'Great Disappointment' came the Seventh-day Adventists (organised 1863), with Ellen G. White as prophetic voice. Charles Taze Russell's Bible Students (from the 1870s) became the Jehovah's Witnesses under that name in 1931. Mary Baker Eddy founded Christian Science (1879).",
 "Other movements stayed within historic Christianity. The Restoration movement of Barton Stone and Alexander Campbell sought to restore New Testament Christianity without creeds, producing the Churches of Christ and the Disciples of Christ. In England the Oxford Movement (from 1833), led by John Keble, John Henry Newman and Edward Pusey, emphasised the Church of England's Catholic heritage; Newman became a Catholic in 1845. The Plymouth Brethren, through John Nelson Darby, developed dispensationalism. William and Catherine Booth founded the Salvation Army (named 1878).",
 "Most Christian churches do not regard the Latter-day Saints, Jehovah's Witnesses or Christian Science as orthodox, chiefly because they reject or redefine the doctrine of the Trinity. The Latter-day Saints and Witnesses have become global movements with millions of members.")

d("xiv-6", "From Reimarus to the Fundamentals",
 "Historical criticism of the Bible grew out of seventeenth-century questions (Spinoza, Richard Simon) about the authorship of the Pentateuch. In Germany, Lessing published fragments by Hermann Samuel Reimarus (1774–1778) arguing that the disciples invented the resurrection. David Friedrich Strauss's Life of Jesus (1835) treated the Gospel miracles as myth and cost him his career. Julius Wellhausen's Prolegomena (1878) gave the classic form of the documentary hypothesis, which saw the Pentateuch as composed from four sources (J, E, D, P), with the priestly law late rather than early.",
 "Liberal Protestant theology responded by locating religion in experience or ethics. Friedrich Schleiermacher defined religion as the feeling of absolute dependence; Albrecht Ritschl and Adolf von Harnack (What is Christianity?, 1900) stressed the kingdom of God as an ethical community and the fatherhood of God, and saw later dogma as a Hellenisation of the Gospel.",
 "Conservative Protestants defended the inspiration and authority of Scripture. At Princeton, Charles Hodge and B. B. Warfield formulated the doctrine of inerrancy in the original manuscripts. The Fundamentals (1910–1915), a series of essays, gave its name to the fundamentalist movement, and the fundamentalist-modernist controversy divided American Presbyterians and Baptists in the 1920s.",
 "In the Catholic Church, scholars such as Alfred Loisy were condemned in the modernist crisis: Pius X's encyclical Pascendi (1907) and the anti-modernist oath (1910) restricted critical scholarship until Pius XII's Divino afflante Spiritu (1943) cautiously accepted historical methods.")

d("xiv-7", "The council, the vote and the Old Catholics",
 "Pius IX opened the First Vatican Council on 8 December 1869 with about 700 bishops. The constitution Dei Filius (April 1870) dealt with faith and reason, affirming that God can be known with certainty by natural reason and condemning rationalism and fideism.",
 "The council then moved, against the wishes of a sizeable minority, to the question of papal primacy and infallibility. Opponents included bishops who thought a definition inopportune, such as Dupanloup of Orléans, and some who rejected it on historical grounds. At a trial vote on 13 July, 451 voted placet, 88 non placet and 62 placet iuxta modum. About 60 of the minority left Rome rather than vote against the pope in public. On 18 July 1870 Pastor aeternus passed 533 to 2 (see the full text of the definition).",
 "The next day the Franco-Prussian War began. French troops protecting Rome were withdrawn; Italian troops entered the city on 20 September 1870 and the Papal States came to an end. The council was suspended and never resumed. Popes regarded themselves as 'prisoners in the Vatican' until the Lateran Treaty with Italy in 1929 created Vatican City.",
 "The church historian Ignaz von Döllinger refused to accept the decree and was excommunicated. Groups in Germany, Switzerland and Austria formed the Old Catholic Churches, which joined the older Church of Utrecht in the Union of Utrecht (1889). Since 1931 they have been in full communion with the Church of England.")

d("xv-1", "Churches under Nazism",
 "Most German Protestants welcomed Hitler's appointment in 1933. The 'German Christians' movement sought a Reich Church that would combine Christianity with Nazi racial ideology and exclude Christians of Jewish descent from the ministry. In response, the Pastors' Emergency League (Martin Niemöller) and then the Confessing Church formed. Its Barmen Declaration (May 1934), drafted largely by Karl Barth, rejected the idea that the Church should acknowledge any revelation besides Jesus Christ or submit to any other lord. It said little about the persecution of Jews.",
 "The Catholic Church signed a concordat with the Reich in July 1933, which protected Catholic institutions on paper in exchange for clerical withdrawal from politics. As violations multiplied, Pius XI issued the encyclical Mit brennender Sorge (1937), smuggled into Germany and read from pulpits. Bishop Clemens von Galen of Münster publicly denounced the euthanasia programme in 1941.",
 "Individual Christians resisted. Dietrich Bonhoeffer, a Lutheran theologian involved in the conspiracy against Hitler, was executed at Flossenbürg in April 1945. Others, including the members of the White Rose and many priests in Dachau, were killed. Many Christians across Europe sheltered Jews; others took part in persecution.",
 "The role of Pius XII remains debated: critics argue he should have spoken out clearly against the Holocaust; defenders point to quiet efforts to save Jews, especially in Rome. The Vatican archives for his pontificate were opened in 2020, and research is ongoing. After the war, Christian reckoning with the theological roots of antisemitism led to the Seelisberg conference (1947), Vatican II's Nostra aetate (1965), and many Protestant statements rejecting the idea that the Jews were rejected by God.")

d("xv-2", "Persecution, survival and revival under communism",
 "Soviet policy toward religion aimed at its eventual elimination. The 1918 decree separated church and state and confiscated church property; the campaign to seize church valuables in 1922 was used to attack the clergy. Patriarch Tikhon was imprisoned. In the late 1930s the Great Terror killed tens of thousands of clergy; by 1939 only a few hundred Orthodox churches were open in the whole country.",
 "In 1943 Stalin, needing support in the war, allowed the election of a patriarch and the reopening of churches and seminaries under strict control. Khrushchev's campaign (1959–1964) closed about half of them again. In Eastern Europe after 1945 regimes imprisoned bishops such as Cardinal Mindszenty in Hungary and Cardinal Stepinac in Yugoslavia, suppressed Greek Catholic churches, and infiltrated church structures with informers. Albania declared itself the world's first atheist state in 1967.",
 "Poland was the exception: the Catholic Church remained a mass institution independent of the state. The election of Karol Wojtyła as John Paul II in 1978 and his visit to Poland in 1979 helped inspire the Solidarity movement. Churches played a part in the 1989 revolutions, notably the peace prayers at St Nicholas Church in Leipzig.",
 "In China the government created state-supervised bodies: the Three-Self Patriotic Movement for Protestants (1954) and the Catholic Patriotic Association (1957). During the Cultural Revolution all public worship stopped. Since 1979 Christianity has grown rapidly, much of it in unregistered 'house churches'. A provisional agreement between the Vatican and China on the appointment of bishops was signed in 2018 and renewed since.")

d("xv-3", "How Pentecostalism became global",
 "Pentecostalism traces itself to revivals around 1900 that taught a 'baptism in the Holy Spirit' after conversion, evidenced by speaking in tongues. Charles Parham's Bible school in Topeka (1901) formulated this teaching. The Azusa Street revival in Los Angeles (1906–1909), led by the African American preacher William J. Seymour, drew Black and white worshippers together and sent missionaries worldwide within months. Parallel revivals occurred in Wales (1904–1905), India (Mukti mission, 1905) and Korea (1907).",
 "Denominations soon formed, among them the Church of God in Christ, the Assemblies of God (1914) and the Pentecostal Assemblies of the World. A dispute over the Trinity produced the 'Oneness' Pentecostals (from 1913).",
 "From the 1960s charismatic renewal brought Pentecostal-style worship into older churches: an Episcopal parish in California in 1960, and Catholic students at Duquesne University in 1967. In the Global South many independent churches developed their own forms, such as the African Initiated Churches (the Kimbanguists in Congo, the Zion Christian Church in South Africa, the Aladura churches in Nigeria) and large neo-Pentecostal churches in Brazil, Nigeria and Korea. The 'prosperity gospel' associated with some of them is criticised by many Pentecostals as well as others.",
 "Estimates of the number of Pentecostals and charismatics vary widely depending on definitions, from around 300 million to over 600 million. It is the fastest-growing form of Christianity of the past century. Evangelicals more broadly organised internationally through the Lausanne Movement (1974).")

d("xv-4", "From Edinburgh 1910 to the Joint Declaration",
 "The modern ecumenical movement is usually dated from the World Missionary Conference at Edinburgh in 1910, where Protestant missionaries concluded that division hindered mission. Two streams followed: Life and Work (Stockholm 1925), on social questions, and Faith and Order (Lausanne 1927), on doctrine. They merged in the World Council of Churches, founded at Amsterdam in 1948. The Orthodox churches joined, many in 1961; the Catholic Church is not a member but takes part in Faith and Order.",
 "Faith and Order's most influential text is Baptism, Eucharist and Ministry (Lima, 1982), which recorded significant convergence. United churches were formed in several countries: the United Church of Canada (1925), the Church of South India (1947), which united Anglicans, Methodists and Reformed with bishops, and the Church of North India (1970).",
 "Vatican II's decree Unitatis redintegratio (1964) committed the Catholic Church to ecumenism and recognised other Christians as brothers and sisters in Christ through baptism. Bilateral dialogues followed with Anglicans (ARCIC, from 1970), Lutherans, Methodists, Reformed, Orthodox and the Oriental churches.",
 "The best-known result is the Joint Declaration on the Doctrine of Justification signed by the Lutheran World Federation and the Catholic Church in Augsburg on 31 October 1999. It states a 'consensus in basic truths' and that the sixteenth-century condemnations do not apply to the teaching presented in it. The World Methodist Council (2006), the Anglican Communion (2016) and the World Communion of Reformed Churches (2017) later associated themselves with it.")

d("xv-5", "What Vatican II changed",
 "John XXIII announced a council in January 1959. The Second Vatican Council met in four autumn sessions from 1962 to 1965 with about 2,500 bishops, many from Africa, Asia and Latin America, and Protestant and Orthodox observers. Paul VI concluded it.",
 "It produced sixteen documents, of which four constitutions are central. Sacrosanctum concilium (1963) on the liturgy called for 'full, conscious and active participation' and allowed the vernacular; the reformed Roman Missal followed in 1969–1970. Lumen gentium (1964) on the Church described it as the people of God, taught the collegiality of bishops with and under the pope, and said that the Church of Christ 'subsists in' the Catholic Church while elements of sanctification exist outside it. Dei Verbum (1965) on revelation treated Scripture and Tradition as one source flowing from God and encouraged biblical study. Gaudium et spes (1965) addressed the Church in the modern world.",
 "Other decrees transformed relations outside the Church: Unitatis redintegratio on ecumenism, Nostra aetate on non-Christian religions, which repudiated the charge that the Jews were collectively guilty of Christ's death, and Dignitatis humanae on religious freedom, which reversed the nineteenth-century rejection of it.",
 "Interpretation of the council has been contested ever since: a 'hermeneutic of continuity' (Benedict XVI) versus readings that stress a new beginning, and a traditionalist minority, notably Archbishop Marcel Lefebvre's Society of St Pius X, which rejected parts of it.")

d("xv-6", "Orthodoxy's modern questions",
 "Twentieth-century emigration created Orthodox communities in Western Europe, North America and Australia that do not fit the traditional rule of one bishop per city. Several jurisdictions overlap in the same places, and the question of who has authority over this 'diaspora' remains disputed between Constantinople and other churches.",
 "Russian émigré theologians in Paris, including Sergei Bulgakov, Vladimir Lossky, Georges Florovsky and Alexander Schmemann (who later moved to New York), introduced Orthodox theology to the West. Florovsky's 'neo-patristic synthesis' and Schmemann's liturgical theology have influenced Catholics and Protestants as well. The Orthodox Church in America received autocephaly from Moscow in 1970, which Constantinople did not recognise.",
 "A Holy and Great Council, planned for decades, met in Crete in June 2016, but the churches of Antioch, Bulgaria, Georgia and Russia did not attend, so its authority is disputed.",
 "In January 2019 Constantinople granted autocephaly to the Orthodox Church of Ukraine. Moscow, which regards Ukraine as its canonical territory, broke communion with Constantinople in 2018. A few churches have recognised the new church; most have not. Russia's full-scale invasion of Ukraine in 2022 and Patriarch Kirill's support for it have deepened the division. The Ukrainian Orthodox Church that had been linked to Moscow declared its independence in May 2022, though the extent of that independence is contested.")

d("xv-7", "Why Middle Eastern Christianity has shrunk",
 "In 1900 Christians made up perhaps a tenth or more of the population of the region from Egypt to Iran. Today the share is far smaller. The decline came from several causes over a century.",
 "Violence: the massacres of Armenians, Assyrians and Greeks in the Ottoman Empire in 1915–1923 (the Armenian Genocide and the Assyrian Sayfo) destroyed communities across eastern Anatolia. In Iraq the Simele massacre of Assyrians (1933) followed independence. After the 2003 invasion of Iraq, Christians were targeted; the Islamic State's capture of Mosul and the Nineveh Plain in 2014 emptied ancient Christian towns. The Syrian civil war from 2011 displaced large numbers.",
 "Emigration: for over a century Christians, often better educated and with links abroad, have emigrated to the Americas, Europe and Australia. Lower birth rates than their Muslim neighbours also reduced their share. Lebanon, where Christians were a majority in 1932, the date of its only census, now probably has a Christian minority.",
 "Survival: Egypt's Copts remain the largest community, probably around ten million, although they face periodic violence and discrimination. Smaller communities persist in Jordan, Israel and Palestine, Syria and Iraq. Migrant workers have created large new Christian populations in the Gulf states. Churches such as the Syriac Orthodox and the Assyrian Church of the East now have more members in the diaspora than in their homelands.")

d("p4-f", "How the Marian doctrines developed",
 "The New Testament gives Mary a modest but significant role: the annunciation, the birth narratives, Cana, the cross and the upper room. Second-century writers, especially Justin and Irenaeus, developed the parallel between Eve and Mary: Eve's disobedience was undone by Mary's obedience. The Protoevangelium of James (c. 150) told of her birth and childhood and asserted her virginity during and after the birth of Jesus.",
 "The title Theotokos, 'God-bearer', was in use in Alexandria by the early fourth century and was confirmed at Ephesus (431) as a statement about Christ: the one born of Mary is God the Word in the flesh. Her perpetual virginity became general belief and was assumed by later councils. Feasts of her Dormition (falling asleep) spread from Jerusalem and were fixed on 15 August in the Byzantine Empire around 600.",
 "In the West the question of whether Mary was conceived without original sin was long debated: Bernard of Clairvaux and Thomas Aquinas did not accept it; Duns Scotus defended it. Pius IX defined the Immaculate Conception in 1854 (see the definition), and Pius XII defined the Assumption of Mary body and soul into heavenly glory in 1950 (Munificentissimus Deus), leaving open whether she died first.",
 "The Orthodox venerate Mary intensely and believe in her Dormition and bodily translation, but do not accept the Immaculate Conception, which they see as unnecessary given their understanding of original sin, nor papal definitions. Luther, Zwingli and Calvin all accepted her perpetual virginity and honoured her as Theotokos; later Protestants largely dropped Marian devotion, though ecumenical dialogues (for example the Anglican–Roman Catholic Mary: Grace and Hope in Christ, 2005) have revisited it.")

d("p4-g", "Christians and Muslims: encounters and arguments",
 "Islam emerged in the seventh century in an Arabian environment that included Christians and Jews. The Qur'an honours Jesus (Isa) as a prophet and Messiah born of the virgin Mary, but denies that he is God's son and, in most readings, that he was crucified (4:157). Christian communities of Syria, Iraq and Egypt came under Muslim rule within a decade of the Prophet's death; they were the majority population in many regions for centuries afterwards.",
 "Early Christian responses treated Islam as a Christian heresy, as John of Damascus did in his Fount of Knowledge, or engaged it in Arabic debate: the Patriarch Timothy I of the Church of the East debated the caliph al-Mahdi around 781, and Christian translators such as Hunayn ibn Ishaq made Greek philosophy and medicine available in Arabic.",
 "Western Europe met Islam chiefly through war (Spain, the Crusades, the Ottoman advance) but also through scholarship: Peter the Venerable of Cluny commissioned the first Latin translation of the Qur'an (1143), and Muslim philosophers shaped scholastic theology. Francis of Assisi met Sultan al-Kamil in Egypt in 1219.",
 "Modern relations were transformed by Vatican II's Nostra aetate (1965), which said that the Church 'regards with esteem' Muslims who 'adore the one God', and by initiatives such as the Muslim scholars' letter A Common Word Between Us and You (2007) and the Document on Human Fraternity signed by Pope Francis and the Grand Imam of al-Azhar in Abu Dhabi (2019).")

d("p4-h", "Christianity and Judaism: from rivalry to dialogue",
 "Christianity began as a movement within Judaism, and its Scriptures include the Jewish Bible. As the two separated (II.7), Christian writers developed a 'teaching of contempt': the claim that the Jews had killed Christ, were rejected by God and had been replaced by the Church (supersessionism). Augustine's view that Jews should be preserved as witnesses to the truth of Scripture, in a subordinate state, shaped medieval policy: Jews were protected from forced conversion in principle by papal bulls (Sicut Judaeis, from about 1120), but subjected to restrictions, accusations and recurrent violence (X.4).",
 "The Reformation did not end this. Luther's late treatise On the Jews and Their Lies (1543) urged burning synagogues; it was later exploited by the Nazis. In Catholic Europe, ghettos were established, beginning in Venice (1516) and in Rome by Paul IV (1555).",
 "After the Holocaust, many churches concluded that Christian anti-Judaism had prepared the ground for modern racial antisemitism. The Seelisberg conference (1947) proposed ten points for Christian teaching. Vatican II's Nostra aetate (1965) stated that what happened in Christ's passion 'cannot be charged against all the Jews, without distinction, then alive, nor against the Jews of today', and that the Jews should not be presented as rejected or accursed. John Paul II visited the Rome synagogue in 1986 and prayed at the Western Wall in 2000. The Vatican and Israel established diplomatic relations in 1993.",
 "Protestant churches made similar statements, including the Evangelical Church in the Rhineland (1980). Jewish scholars responded in Dabru Emet (2000). Questions still under discussion include mission to Jews, the theological meaning of the State of Israel, and how Christians should read New Testament texts critical of 'the Jews'.")

d("p4-j", "Models of church and state",
 "Christianity has lived under almost every possible relation to political power. Before Constantine it was an illegal minority that nonetheless prayed for the emperor (Romans 13; 1 Timothy 2). After Theodosius it became the established religion of the Roman Empire, and the Byzantine ideal of 'symphony' (Justinian, Novel 6) described emperor and priesthood as two gifts of God working in harmony.",
 "In the West, Gelasius I's letter of 494 distinguished the 'sacred authority of bishops' from the 'royal power' (VII.4). The medieval papacy built on this to claim superiority over rulers, culminating in Unam sanctam (1302). Kings and emperors in turn claimed authority over their churches, and the investiture conflict (X.1) was fought over this boundary.",
 "The Reformation produced several models: Lutheran territorial churches under princely authority; Calvin's Geneva, where church and magistrate were distinct but cooperated in discipline; the Church of England under royal supremacy; and the Anabaptist rejection of the sword altogether. The principle cuius regio, eius religio (1555) made the ruler's faith that of the territory.",
 "Modern models include separation (the United States, 1791; France's law of 1905 on laïcité), concordats (Napoleon in 1801; the Lateran Treaty of 1929), established national churches (England, Denmark, until 2000 Sweden), the Orthodox pattern of national churches closely tied to national identity, and authoritarian control (the Soviet Union, China). Vatican II's Dignitatis humanae (1965) accepted religious freedom as a right grounded in human dignity, which ended Catholic support for confessional states in principle.")
